ist\u003cBytesReference\u003e values \u003d dimensions.get(name);\n+            if (values \u003d\u003d null) {\n+                // optimize for the common case where dimensions are not multi-valued\n+                dimensions.put(name, List.of(encoded));\n+            } else {\n+                if (values.size() \u003d\u003d 1) {\n+                    // converts the immutable list that\u0027s optimized for the common case of having only one value to a mutable list\n+                    BytesReference previousValue \u003d values.get(0);\n+                    values \u003d new ArrayList\u003c\u003e(4);","submitted_line":389},"generatedCommentsNodes":[{"comment":"The changes in these diffs replace an exception check with a test that expects multi-valued fields to be parsed successfully and then checks the size of the resulting document fields. This change seems to contradict the original intent of ensuring dimension fields are not multi-valued. The tests should either revert to checking for exceptions or the logic allowing multi-valued fields should be confirmed as correct. Additionally, the refactoring of `TimeSeriesIdFieldMapper.TimeSeriesIdBuilder` to use a `SortedMap\u003cBytesRef, List\u003cBytesReference\u003e\u003e` instead of a `SortedSet\u003cDimension\u003e` allows for multi-valued dimensions. However, the existing logic in methods like `buildLegacyTsid`, `buildTsidHash`, and `add` needs to be updated to handle multi-valued dimensions correctly. Ensure that these changes do not introduce any regressions or incorrect behavior.","nodes":[{"id":"server/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/index/mapper/flattened/FlattenedFieldMapperTests.java-server/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/index/mapper/flattened/FlattenedFieldMapperTests.java-9422:9591,9600:9717-9494:9719,9728:9802","promptId":"#N3VH8","srcPath":"server/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/index/mapper/flattened/FlattenedFieldMapperTests.java","dstPath":"server/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/index/mapper/flatt