arrays. This is why in the `compare to` method in `NameAndHash`, we\u0027re primarily sorting by the name, and the order is just the tie breaker, which only affects arrays, as that\u0027s the only way you can have two `NameAndHash` instances with the same name. This is also tested in `org.elasticsearch.cluster.routing.IndexRoutingTests#testRoutingPathArraysInSource`."],"path":"server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/cluster/routing/IndexRouting.java","id":36665,"created_at":"2024-09-13T08:07:57Z","original_position":47,"side":"RIGHT","position":88,"submitted_on_commit":"30d334b9786bd18fada3857d6bc76a35e049711f","diff_hunk":"@@ -402,6 +400,10 @@ private void extractItem(String path, XContentParser source) throws IOException\n                 }\n             }\n \n+            private void addHash(String path, BytesRef value) {\n+                hashes.add(new NameAndHash(new BytesRef(path), hash(value), hashes.size()));","submitted_line":404},"generatedCommentsNodes":[]}],"coveredGroundTruth":1,"uncoveredGroundTruth":1,"coveredMatchable":20,"uncoveredMatchable":25,"timing":56696,"tokensIn":16547,"tokensOut":1398}